[claude-9b] Released in setup tool 2.23.0 (2026-10-28) with its default (off, today's behavior), tested by
claude-9b-test in s157 (in every scene; on top of 0145's settings it showed no gain, so it stays off). The text below
and the "UNTESTED" marks in the code comments are from the draft.
[claude-9b-think] 2026-10-27 draft. SDK patch 0148: Vulkan shader translation off the GPU
thread, as the D3D12 backend does. On top of 0145 v2 (0145-vulkan-async-pipelines-without-placeholder-v2). Owner
19:31: "optimizting in-game performance and reducing/eliminating hitching is priority #1".

Why: IssueDraw translates every new shader on the GPU thread while the draw waits (command_processor.cpp
4116-4126 -> VulkanPipelineCache::EnsureShadersTranslated, pipeline_cache.cpp 1084-1123). In the owner's play
today that was 1-5 shaders and 0.7-3.8 ms in the in-play slow frames, and 105-107 shaders, 62-73 ms, at each
resolution-scale switch. With 0145 v2 (no placeholder, no wait at submission ends) translation is the last
compile work a new shader puts on the GPU thread. The D3D12 backend already translates on its pipeline threads
(d3d12/pipeline_cache.cpp 972-985, 1086-1104).

What it does:
  - vulkan_async_shader_translation (default false: as before). On (with async_shader_compilation): a
    translation not made yet is handed to one "Vulkan Shader Translation" thread with its own
    SpirvShaderTranslator (the same settings as the GPU thread's, CreateShaderTranslator), and the draw is
    left out until it is in, the same way as a draw whose pipeline is still being made (0145): IssueDraw
    marks the frame (frame_used_async_placeholder_pipeline_: not shown with
    vulkan_async_skip_incomplete_frames) and counts the draw (draws_left_out_for_pipelines_), then returns.
    The next draw with the shaders goes on to ConfigurePipeline, whose pipeline 0145 makes on the pipeline
    threads: a new shader costs the GPU thread no compile work at all.
  - One translation thread, so the translations of a shader are made one after another: the first fills the
    shader's texture and sampler bindings (SpirvShaderTranslator::PostTranslation, once a shader), and any
    translation seen as done has its shader's bindings complete.
  - A translation's state is a new atomic in VulkanTranslation (0 nothing in flight, 1 queued or being
    translated: only the translation thread touches it; 2 done, not yet seen by the GPU thread). The GPU
    thread reads the translation only in states 0 and 2 (acquire), so there is no lock per draw: one atomic
    load per shader per draw, and a lock only to queue.
  - The binding layout UIDs (the pipeline cache's maps: GPU thread only) are split out of
    TranslateAnalyzedShader into SetUpBindingLayoutUIDs, called there as before and by the GPU thread when it
    first sees a translation the thread made (once per shader, EnterBindingLayoutUserUIDSetup).
  - A translation in flight when the setting is turned off is still finished by the thread (its draws left
    out until then), so no translation is made on two threads.
  - The thread stops before the translations are destroyed (draw resolution scale change) and at shutdown;
    queued translations go back to "nothing in flight" and are asked for again by the next draw. It starts
    with the pipeline threads (Initialize, CreateDrawResolutionScaleDependentObjects).
  - Counters in 0145's "Vulkan pipelines" line: "...; shaders: N handed to the translation thread, N
    translated there (X ms); of the draws left out, N waited for a translation". The GPU thread's own
    translations stay in the slow-frame line's "N shaders translated (X ms)" (gpu_stats), as before.
Not changed: the ucode analysis (on the GPU thread, IssueDraw's AnalyzeShaderUcode, before a translation is
asked for), the shader storage (stored shaders are translated at start as before), D3D12.
Assumption to check at the build: VulkanTranslation::GetOrCreateShaderModule (its .cpp isn't in the build tree
copy read here) only creates a VkShaderModule from the translation (thread-safe in Vulkan). A translation with
no module is treated as failed (RequestShadersTranslated), whatever is_valid says.

Test (s157, after 0145's runs; FINDINGS-HITCHES recipe: cache and driver cache cleared, verbose):
  B: 0145's B (--vulkan_async_pipeline_placeholders=false --vulkan_async_wait_at_submission=false);
  E2: B plus --vulkan_async_shader_translation=true;
  F: E2 plus --vulkan_async_skip_incomplete_frames=false.
  Expect in E2: slow-frame lines' "N shaders translated" 0 (the thread's count in the "Vulkan pipelines" line
  instead), the GPU thread's frame time in first-visit scenes down by 0.7-4 ms per new shader burst, holds a
  little longer (translation, then pipeline). A resolution-scale switch in F2 (1x -> 2x) is the big case
  (107 shaders: 62-73 ms on the GPU thread today).

